== Chapter III

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The second general Principle of the Means of Mortification proposed to Confirmation. The Spirit the only Author of this work. Vanity of Popish Mortification discovered. Many means of it used by them not appointed of God. Those appointed by him abused. The mistakes of others in this business. The Spirit is promised Believers for this work.] Ezek 11:19. ch. 36:26. #emph[All that we receive from Christ is by the Spirit. How the Spirit Mortifies sin,] Gal 5:19, 20, 21, 22, 23. #emph[The several ways of his Operations to this end proposed. How his Work, and our Duty.]]
]

#smallcaps[THE] next Principle relates to the great Sovereign Cause of the #emph[Mortification] treated of, which, in the words laid for the Foundation of this Discourse, is said to be the #emph[Spirit,] that is, the Holy Ghost, as was evinced.

He only is sufficient for this work. All ways and means without him are as a thing of nought; and He is the great Efficient of it, He #emph[works in us, as he pleases.]

1\. In vain do men seek other remedies, they shall not be healed by them. What several ways have been prescribed for this, to have sin #emph[mortified,] is known. The greatest part of #emph[Popish Religion,] of that which looks most like Religion in their Profession, consists in mistaken Ways and Means of Mortification. This is the pretense of their #emph[rough garments,] whereby they deceive. Their #emph[Vows, Orders, Fastings, Penances,] are all built on this ground, they are all for the mortifying of Sin. Their #emph[Preachings, Sermons,] and #emph[Books] of Devotion, they look all this way. Hence those who interpret the #emph[Locusts] that came out of the #emph[bottomless pit,] Rev 9:3, to be the #emph[Friars] of the #emph[Romish Church,] who are said to torment men, so #emph[that they should seek death and not find it,] v. 6 think, that they did it by their stinging #emph[Sermons,] whereby they convinced them of Sin, but being not able to discover the Remedy for the healing and Mortifying of it, they kept them in perpetual Anguish and Terror, and such trouble in their Consciences, that they #emph[desired to die.] This I say is the substance and Glory of their Religion: but what with their laboring to #emph[mortify] dead Creatures, ignorant of the Nature and End of the work, what with the Poison they mixed with it, in their persuasion of its Merit, yea Supererogation, (as they style their #emph[unnecessary merit,] with a proud barbarous title) their glory is their shame; but of them and their #emph[Mortification,] more afterwards: ch. 7.

That the #emph[Ways] and #emph[Means] to be used for the Mortification of sin, #emph[invented] by them, are still #emph[insisted on and prescribed] for the same end by some, who should have more #emph[light] and Knowledge of the Gospel, is known. Such Directions to this purpose have of late been given by some, and are greedily #emph[catched] at by #emph[others] professing themselves #emph[Protestants,] as might have become #emph[Popish Devotionists] three or four hundred years ago. Such outside Endeavors, such bodily Exercises, such self-performances, such merely #emph[Legal Duties,] without the least mention of #emph[Christ,] or his Spirit, are #emph[varnished] over with swelling words of vanity, for the only Means and #emph[Expedients] for the Mortification of sin, as discover a deep rooted unacquaintedness with the power of God, and Mystery of the Gospel. The consideration hereof, was one Motive to the publishing of this plain Discourse.

Now the Reasons why the #emph[Papists] can never with all their Endeavors truly mortify any one sin, amongst others, are,

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Because many of the Ways and Means they use and insist upon for this End, were never #emph[appointed] of God for that purpose. Now there is nothing in Religion that hath any Efficacy for compassing an End, but it hath it from Gods Appointment of it to that purpose. Such as these are, their #emph[rough Garments,] their #emph[Vows,] Penances, Disciplines, their Course of #emph[Monastical] Life, and the like, concerning all which God will say, #emph[Who hath required these things at your hands?] and #emph[In vain do you worship me, teaching for Doctrines the Traditions of men.] Of the same Nature are sundry #emph[self-vexations,] insisted on by others.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Because those things that are appointed of #emph[God] as #emph[Means,] are not used by them in their due Place and Order: such as are #emph[Praying, Fasting, Watching, Meditation,] and the like; these have their use in the business in hand. But whereas they are all to be looked on as #emph[streams,] they look on them as the #emph[fountain.] Whereas they #emph[effect] and accomplish the End as Means only subordinate to the #emph[Spirit] and Faith, they look on them to do it by virtue of the work wrought. If they #emph[fast] so much, and #emph[pray] so much, and keep their #emph[hours] and times, the work is done: As the Apostle says of some in another case, they are always #emph[learning,] never coming to the #emph[knowledge of the Truth]\; so they are always #emph[mortifying,] but never come to any sound #emph[Mortification.] In a word, they have sundry Means to #emph[mortify] the #emph[Natural man,] as to the Natural #emph[life] here we lead, none to mortify #emph[Lust] or #emph[Corruption.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is the general mistake of men ignorant of the Gospel, about this thing; and it lies at the bottom of very much of that Superstition and Will-worship that hath been brought into the world; what horrible self-macerations were practised by some of the ancient Authors of #emph[Monastical Devotion?] what violence did they offer to Nature? What extremity of sufferings did they put themselves upon? Search their ways and Principles to the bottom, and you will find, that it had no other root but this mistake, namely, that attempting #emph[rigid] Mortification, they fell upon the #emph[natural man,] instead of the corrupt old man; upon the body wherein we live, instead of the Body of Death.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Neither will the #emph[natural Popery] that is in others do it. Men are #emph[galled] with the Guilt of a Sin, that hath prevailed over them: they instantly promise to themselves and #emph[God,] that they will do so no #emph[more]\; they watch over themselves, and pray, for a #emph[season,] until this #emph[heat] waxes cold, and the sense of Sin is #emph[worn] off, and so #emph[Mortification] goes also, and Sin returns to its former #emph[Dominion: Duties] are excellent #emph[food] for an healthy Soul; they are no #emph[physic] for a #emph[sick] Soul. He that turns his #emph[meat] into his #emph[medicine,] must expect no great #emph[operation.] Spiritually #emph[sick] men cannot #emph[sweat] out their #emph[distemper] with #emph[working.] But this is the way of men that deceive their own Souls; as we shall see afterwards.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[That none of these ways are sufficient, is evident from the Nature of the work itself that is to be done; it is a work that requires so many concurrent actings in it, as no self-#emph[Endeavor] can reach unto; and is of that kind, that an Almighty #emph[Energy] is necessary for its #emph[accomplishment,] as shall be afterwards manifested.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is then the work of the Spirit. For,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. He is #emph[Promised] of God to be given unto us, to do this work; the taking away of the #emph[stony] heart, that is, the #emph[stubborn, proud, rebellious, unbelieving] Heart, is in general the work of Mortification that we treat of. Now this is still promised to be done by the Spirit: Ezek 11:19. ch. 36:26. #emph[I will give my Spirit, and take away the stony heart:] and by the #emph[Spirit] of God is this work wrought, when all Means fail. Isa 57:17, 18.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. We have all our #emph[Mortification] from the #emph[Gift] of Christ, and all the Gifts of Christ are #emph[communicated] to us, and given us by the #emph[Spirit] of Christ. #emph[Without Christ we can do nothing,] John 15:5. All #emph[communications] of Supplies and Relief in the #emph[beginnings, increasings, actings] of any Grace whatever from him, are by the Spirit, by whom he alone #emph[works] in and upon Believers. From him we have our #emph[Mortification: He is exalted and made a Prince and a Savior, to give Repentance unto us:] Acts 5:31 and of our Repentance our #emph[Mortification] is no small Portion. How doth he do it? Having received the Promise of the Holy Ghost, he sends him abroad for that end: Acts 2:33. You know the manifold Promises he made of sending the Spirit, as #emph[Tertullian] speaks, #emph[vicariam navare operam,] to do the #emph[Works] that he had to accomplish in us.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The Resolution of one or two #emph[Questions] will now lead me nearer to what I principally intend.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The first is,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Q. #emph[How doth the Spirit mortify Sin?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I Answer, in general three ways.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] 1. By causing our hearts to abound in #emph[Grace,] and the #emph[Fruits] that are contrary to the Flesh, and the Fruits thereof, and Principles of them. So the Apostle opposes the Fruits of the Flesh, and of the Spirit: The Fruits of the Flesh (says he) are so and so, Gal 5:19, 20 but says he, the Fruits of the Spirit are quite contrary, quite of another sort, vv. 22, 23. Yea, but what if these are in us, and do #emph[abound,] may not the other #emph[abound] also? No, says he, v. 24. #emph[They that are Christ's have crucified the flesh, with the affections and lusts:] But how? Why, v. 25 by #emph[living in the Spirit, and walking after the Spirit]: That is, by the abounding of these Graces of the Spirit in us, and walking according to them. For, saith the Apostle, these are #emph[contrary one to another,] v. 17 so that they cannot both be in the same subject, in any intense or high degree. This Renewing of us by the Holy Ghost, as it is called, Titus 3:5 is one great way of #emph[Mortification:] He causes us to #emph[grow, thrive, flourish,] and abound in those Graces which are #emph[contrary, opposite,] and destructive to all the #emph[fruits] of the Flesh, and to the quiet, or #emph[thriving] of indwelling #emph[sin] itself.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. By a #emph[real, physical] Efficiency on the Root and Habit of Sin, for the weakening, destroying and taking it away. Hence he is called a #emph[Spirit of Judgment and Burning,] Isa 4:4 really consuming and destroying our Lusts. He takes away the #emph[stony heart] by an #emph[Almighty] Efficiency; for as he #emph[begins] the work as to its #emph[kind,] so he #emph[carries] it on as to its #emph[degrees.] He is the Fire which #emph[burns] up the very #emph[root] of Lust.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. He brings the #emph[cross] of Christ into the Heart of a Sinner by Faith, and gives us Communion with Christ in his #emph[Death,] and #emph[Fellowship] in his sufferings; of the #emph[manner] whereof more afterwards.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Q.] If this be the #emph[work of the Spirit alone,] how is it, that we are exhorted to it? Seeing the Spirit of God only can do it, let the work be left wholly to him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] 1. It is no otherwise the work of the Spirit, but as all Graces and good #emph[Works] which are in us, are his: He works in us #emph[to will and to do of his own good pleasure,] Phil 2:13. He works all our #emph[works in us,] Isa 26:12. #emph[the work of Faith with power]\; 2 Thess 1:11. Col 2:12. He causes us to #emph[pray,] and is #emph[a Spirit of Supplication,] Rom 8:26. Zech 12:10 and yet we are #emph[exhorted,] and are to be exhorted to all these.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. He doth not so work our #emph[Mortification] in us, as not to keep it still an Act of our #emph[Obedience.] The Holy Ghost works #emph[in] us, and #emph[upon] us, as we are fit to be wrought #emph[in,] and #emph[upon,] that is, so as to preserve our own #emph[liberty,] and free Obedience. He works upon our #emph[Understandings, Wills, Consciences,] and #emph[Affections,] agreeably to their own Natures; He works #emph[in us,] and #emph[with us,] not #emph[against] us, or #emph[without] us; so that his Assistance is an Encouragement, as to the #emph[facilitating] of the Work, and no Occasion of #emph[neglect] as to the work itself. And indeed I might here bewail the endless #emph[foolish] labor of poor Souls, who being convinced of sin, and not able to stand against the Power of their Convictions, do set themselves by innumerable #emph[perplexing] Ways and Duties to keep #emph[down] sin, but being strangers to the #emph[Spirit] of God, all in vain. They #emph[combat] without Victory, have #emph[War] without #emph[Peace,] and are in slavery all their days. They spend their #emph[strength] for that which is not #emph[bread,] and their #emph[labor] for that which #emph[profiteth] not.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is the saddest warfare that any poor Creature can be engaged in; A Soul under the #emph[power] of Conviction from the Law, is pressed to fight against Sin, but hath no strength for the Combat. They cannot but #emph[fight,] and they can never conquer, they are like men thrust on the Sword of Enemies, on purpose to be slain. The #emph[Law] drives them on, and Sin beats them back. Sometimes they think indeed that they have foiled sin; when they have only raised #emph[a dust,] that they see it not; that is, they distemper their natural Affections of Fear, Sorrow, and Anguish, which makes them believe that sin is #emph[conquered,] when it is not touched. By that time they are cold, they must to the battle again; and the Lust which they thought to be slain, appears to have had no wound.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And if the Case be so sad with them who do #emph[labor] and strive, and yet enter #emph[not] into the Kingdom; what is their Condition who #emph[despise] all this? Who are #emph[perpetually] under the Power and Dominion of Sin, and love to have it so: and are troubled at nothing but that they cannot make #emph[sufficient provision for] the Flesh, to fulfill the Lusts thereof.]]
